        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Bhakti movement</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where</span><span class="info-stat-val">Arose in the Tamil country of south <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>; spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>, then across northern India, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 6th–17th century: the Tamil <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayanars">Nayanars</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alvars">Alvars</a> c. 6th–9th centuries, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> in the 12th, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a> from the 13th, northern and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> above all in the 15th–17th</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Leading figures</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andal">Andal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basava">Basava</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnyaneshwar">Dnyaneshwar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabir">Kabir</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirabai">Mirabai</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaitanya-mahaprabhu">Chaitanya Mahaprabhu</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">Made devotion sung in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>’s regional languages the heart of popular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> and founded lasting traditions, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-vaishnavism">Sri Vaishnavas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingayatism">Lingayats</a> to the Varkaris and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaudiya-vaishnavism">Gaudiya Vaishnavas</a></span></div>
