    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Beginning among Tamil poet-saints in the 6th to 9th centuries, the bhakti movement taught that loving devotion to a personal god is the surest path to liberation, open to all whatever their birth.</li><li>Its saints, women and low-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> artisans as well as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a>, sang in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>’s spoken languages, not <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a>, and their songs shaped literatures from Tamil and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a>, Hindi and Bengali.</li><li>Never one organisation, it spread region by region over a thousand years, made devotion the centre of popular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a>, and some of its saints’ songs are preserved in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> scripture.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tulsidas">Tulsidas</a> recorded in the Ramcharitmanas itself that he began the poem at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayodhya">Ayodhya</a> in 1574, on the festival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a>’s birth; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramlila">Ramlila</a> plays still staged across northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> each autumn are based on its verses.</p></aside>
    </section>

