    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 6th–9th century</span><span class="rf-text">The Tamil <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayanars">Nayanars</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alvars">Alvars</a> compose hymns to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> and sing them at temples across the south</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">10th century</span><span class="rf-text">Nathamuni gathers the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alvars">Alvars</a>’ hymns as the Nalayira Divya Prabandham</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">12th century</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basava">Basava</a>, minister at the Kalachuri court of Kalyani, leads the Virashaiva or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingayatism">Lingayat</a> movement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1290</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnyaneshwar">Dnyaneshwar</a> completes the Dnyaneshwari, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a> verse commentary on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagavad-gita">Bhagavad Gita</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">15th century</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabir">Kabir</a>, a weaver of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a>, sings of a formless God beyond the rites of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> and Muslims</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1510</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaitanya-mahaprabhu">Chaitanya Mahaprabhu</a> becomes a renunciant and settles at Puri, spreading the chanting of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>’s names</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1574</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tulsidas">Tulsidas</a> begins the Ramcharitmanas, the story of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a> retold in the Awadhi form of Hindi</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1650</span><span class="rf-text">Death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tukaram">Tukaram</a>, whose songs crown the Varkari tradition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bhagavad-gita"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bhagavad-gita.jpg" alt="Bhagavad Gita" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bhagavad-gita">Bhagavad Gita</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BhagavadGita-19th-century-Illustrated-Sanskrit-Chapter_1.20.21.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown artist, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The word bhakti comes from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> root meaning to share in or to belong to, and came to mean loving devotion to a god. The idea was old. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagavad-gita">Bhagavad Gita</a>, probably composed in the last centuries BCE, presents devotion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> as a path to liberation beside the paths of ritual action and knowledge, and promises that women and people of low birth who take refuge in him also reach the highest goal. In the following centuries the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puranas">Puranas</a> told the stories of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> and the goddess, and temples to them multiplied under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Guptas</a> and their successors. Religious authority, however, still rested largely with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> priests and scholars, who performed the rites and guarded the Sanskrit scriptures, and with ascetic orders, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a>, Jain and Buddhist, who taught that release came through renunciation.</p><p>Bhakti as a popular movement began in the Tamil country of the far south, which from the late 6th century was divided between the Pallava kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Kanchipuram</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandyas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a>. Jain and Buddhist monks there enjoyed the patronage of kings and merchants. Against them arose wandering poet-saints who went from temple to temple singing of the god who dwelt in each: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayanars">Nayanars</a>, devotees of Shiva, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alvars">Alvars</a>, devotees of Vishnu. They drew on the classical Tamil poetry of the Sangam age, whose verses of love and separation they turned into the longing of the soul for God, and they sang in Tamil, not Sanskrit, to any who would listen.</p><p>Over the following thousand years devotional poets arose in region after region, each in its own language. In the north from the 13th century they lived under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>, beside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> orders whose music and mystical poetry of love spoke a similar language, and beside the yogis of the Nath tradition. Some earlier scholars traced bhakti to the influence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> or even of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. Most historians now see its roots in older Indian traditions, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upanishads">Upanishads</a> and the Gita to the Tamil poets, while recognising that in the north saints and Sufis drew on a shared world of ideas and images.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aims">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aims</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/krishna"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/krishna.jpg" alt="Krishna" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Krishna_Holds_Up_Mount_Govardhan_(MET_70694)_cropped.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Miskin, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>At the heart of bhakti was the conviction that God, whether <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> in one of his forms, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a>, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>, could be known and loved as a person, and that such love, freely given and answered by divine grace, led to liberation more surely than sacrifice, learning or austerity. The devotee might approach God as a servant approaches a master, a child its parent, a friend a friend or a lover the beloved. Many poets sang of a particular image in a particular temple: Ranganatha at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srirangam">Srirangam</a>, Vithoba at Pandharpur, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagannath">Jagannath</a> at Puri. Others, the sants of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabir">Kabir</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ravidas">Ravidas</a>, worshipped God without form or attributes, nirguna, whom no image or scripture could contain, in contrast to the saguna devotion to a god with form.</p><p>The means were open to all: singing and repeating the names of God, hearing the songs of the saints, keeping company with fellow devotees, pilgrimage and service to a guru. The Bhagavata Purana, whose final form many scholars date to the 9th or early 10th century and trace to the south, carried this devotion to Krishna into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> and became its great scripture. Teachers gave the devotion a philosophy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramanuja">Ramanuja</a>, traditionally dated 1017–1137, taught at Srirangam that souls and the world are real and form the body of God, a system known as Vishishtadvaita, qualified non-dualism, which he set against the strict non-dualism of Adi Shankara. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhvacharya">Madhvacharya</a> taught that God and souls are for ever distinct. In the 16th century Vallabha preached a path of grace, and Chaitanya’s followers at Vrindavan, above all Rupa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanatana-goswami">Sanatana Goswami</a>, set out a theology that analysed the emotions of love for Krishna.</p><p>The saints spoke and sang in the languages of ordinary people, and many declared that before God birth, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a>, sex and learning counted for nothing. Some went further. The vachanas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basava">Basava</a>’s circle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> rejected caste distinctions and temple ritual, and Kabir mocked the pride of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> and mullah alike. Yet most saints sought a change of heart rather than a new social order, and many of the movement’s teachers were themselves Brahmins who wrote in Sanskrit as well as in the vernaculars. How far the movement set out to challenge caste, or the authority of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a> and of Sanskrit, remains one of the main questions that historians debate.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="events">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Course of Events</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sambandar"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sambandar.jpg" alt="Sambandar" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sambandar">Sambandar</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Hindu_Saint_Sambandar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown (late 14th century artist), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Tamil phase lasted from about the 6th to the 9th century. Among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayanars">Nayanars</a>, Karaikkal Ammaiyar, probably of the 6th century, was one of the earliest; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appar">Appar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sambandar">Sambandar</a> in the 7th century and Sundarar after them composed the hymns later collected as the Tevaram. Among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alvars">Alvars</a>, Nammalvar composed the Tiruvaymoli and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andal">Andal</a> her songs of longing for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>. In the 10th century Nathamuni gathered the Alvars’ hymns as the Nalayira Divya Prabandham, the “four thousand divine compositions”, and the Shaiva hymns were gathered in the Tirumurai, whose saints’ lives Sekkizhar retold in the 12th-century Periya Puranam. Sung in the temples, the hymns became part of their worship.</p><p>From the south the movement spread north. In 12th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basava">Basava</a>, a minister of the Kalachuri king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijjala-ii">Bijjala II</a> at Kalyani, led a community of devotees of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>, the Virashaivas or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingayatism">Lingayats</a>, who expressed their faith in short prose poems called vachanas. Lingayat tradition tells that a marriage within the community between a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a>’s daughter and a cobbler’s son provoked the king’s punishment and violent turmoil, and that the community then scattered. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnyaneshwar">Dnyaneshwar</a> completed the Dnyaneshwari, his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a> commentary on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagavad-gita">Bhagavad Gita</a>, in 1290; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namdev">Namdev</a> and his companions sang of the god Vithoba of Pandharpur, the focus of the Varkari tradition, which Eknath carried on in the 16th century and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tukaram">Tukaram</a>, who died about 1650, in the 17th. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a> the Haridasas, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/purandara-dasa">Purandara Dasa</a>, sang to Vishnu in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a>.</p><p>In the north the 15th and 16th centuries were the great age. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabir">Kabir</a>, a weaver of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a>, sang of a formless God in pithy couplets and songs, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ravidas">Ravidas</a>, a leatherworker of the same city, shared much of his vision. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-nanak">Guru Nanak</a>, the founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhism</a>, taught devotion to one formless God in terms close to theirs, though Sikh tradition regards his teaching as a revelation of its own. Vallabha taught, and Surdas and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirabai">Mirabai</a> sang, devotion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>, and in 1574 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tulsidas">Tulsidas</a> began the Ramcharitmanas. In the east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaitanya-mahaprabhu">Chaitanya Mahaprabhu</a> took the vows of a renunciant in 1510 and spent his later years at Puri, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srimanta-sankardev">Srimanta Sankardev</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ekasarana-dharma">Ekasarana Dharma</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a>. In 1604 Guru Arjan, the fifth Sikh Guru, placed hymns of Kabir, Namdev, Ravidas and other saints in the Adi Granth.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="people">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">People</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mirabai"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mirabai.jpg" alt="Mirabai" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mirabai">Mirabai</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kangra_painting_of_Mirabai,_the_female_Bhakti_saint.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The poet-saints came from every level of society. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andal">Andal</a>, probably of the 8th century and the only woman among the twelve <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alvars">Alvars</a>, wrote the Tiruppavai, still sung in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> temples in the Tamil month of Margazhi, in December and January; tradition holds that she would marry no one but the god himself and was united with him as Ranganatha at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srirangam">Srirangam</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appar">Appar</a>, born a Shaiva, became a Jain monk before returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>, and tradition credits him with converting the Pallava king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahendravarman-i">Mahendravarman I</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basava">Basava</a>’s circle Akka Mahadevi left home to wander as a devotee of Shiva, whom she called Chennamallikarjuna, rendered by the translator A. K. Ramanujan as “lord white as jasmine”.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namdev">Namdev</a> came from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> of tailors, Janabai was a maidservant in his household, Gora a potter and Chokhamela a Mahar, of a caste then treated as untouchable, who, tradition says, was kept outside the temple of the god he praised. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tukaram">Tukaram</a>, a small trader of Dehu from a family outside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> castes, made enemies by attacking hypocrisy; tradition says that he was made to sink his poems in the Indrayani river and that they rose again unharmed. In the north <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabir">Kabir</a> grew up in a family of Muslim weavers, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ravidas">Ravidas</a> was a leatherworker and Dadu Dayal a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>-carder. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirabai">Mirabai</a>, by contrast, was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> princess who, tradition holds, defied her royal in-laws to sing and dance before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tulsidas">Tulsidas</a> was a Brahmin scholar, and Chaitanya, also a Brahmin, was a young teacher of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabadwip">Nabadwip</a> before devotion overwhelmed him.</p><p>Behind the famous names stood countless followers: the singers who carried the songs from village to village, the families who walked each year to Pandharpur, the temple musicians, the monks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingayatism">Lingayat</a> mathas and of the sattras that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srimanta-sankardev">Sankardev</a>’s disciples founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a>, and the patrons, kings, nobles and merchants, who built shrines and fed pilgrims. Women were prominent among both saints and listeners. Most songs lived first in memory and performance and were written down later, so that the work of a famous poet cannot always be separated from that of the singers who added to it in his or her name.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="outcomes">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Outcomes</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/chaitanya-mahaprabhu"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chaitanya-mahaprabhu.jpg" alt="Chaitanya Mahaprabhu" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/chaitanya-mahaprabhu">Chaitanya Mahaprabhu</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chaitanya_dances_with_followers.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The movement transformed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> religious life. Devotion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>, expressed in temple worship, festivals, pilgrimage and congregational singing, became the centre of popular Hinduism across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. In the Tamil country the revival of Shaiva and Vaishnava devotion went together with the decline of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, which lost the favour of kings; later Shaiva accounts of debates won against Jain monks and of the impalement of the losers at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a> are treated by most historians as legend rather than record. Lasting communities grew from the movement: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-vaishnavism">Sri Vaishnavas</a>, the Tamil Shaivas of the Shaiva Siddhanta, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingayatism">Lingayats</a>, the Varkaris of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>, the followers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhvacharya">Madhva</a> and Vallabha, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaudiya-vaishnavism">Gaudiya Vaishnavas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabir">Kabir</a> Panth and the Ekasarana tradition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a>.</p><p>It also created literatures. The hymns of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayanars">Nayanars</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alvars">Alvars</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> vachanas, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a> Dnyaneshwari and the abhangas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namdev">Namdev</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tukaram">Tukaram</a>, Kabir’s couplets, the Ramcharitmanas, the songs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirabai">Mirabai</a> and Surdas, the Bengali songs of Chaitanya’s followers and the plays and songs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srimanta-sankardev">Sankardev</a> in Assam are among the founding works of the modern languages of India. Devotion shaped music as well: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/purandara-dasa">Purandara Dasa</a> is remembered as the father of Carnatic music, and devotional songs remain a large part of both classical and popular music.</p><p>The movement’s social challenge had clear limits. The saints taught that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> meant nothing before God, but caste itself survived, and some of the communities born in protest became castes in their turn. Saints born in low castes were revered, yet people of their birth remained barred from many temples, including Vithoba’s at Pandharpur, until the 20th century. Women saints were honoured, but often as exceptions to the rules that bound other women. As the communities hardened into sects with their own lineages of teachers, rituals and properties, the creative age of the poet-saints passed, although devotional poets continued to appear in the 18th century and after.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/rabindranath-tagore"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rabindranath-tagore.jpg" alt="Rabindranath Tagore" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/rabindranath-tagore">Rabindranath Tagore</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1926_Rabindrath_Tagore.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Georges Chevalier, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The songs of the bhakti saints remain part of daily life in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Each year hundreds of thousands of Varkari pilgrims walk to Pandharpur, escorting palanquins that carry the sandals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnyaneshwar">Dnyaneshwar</a> from Alandi and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tukaram">Tukaram</a> from Dehu. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramlila">Ramlila</a> plays follow the Ramcharitmanas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andal">Andal</a>’s verses are sung each Margazhi, and the Guru Granth Sahib, the scripture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhism</a>, preserves the hymns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabir">Kabir</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namdev">Namdev</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ravidas">Ravidas</a> and other saints, and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> Shaikh Farid, beside those of the Sikh Gurus. The International Society for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> Consciousness, founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> in 1966 by A. C. Bhaktivedanta Swami Prabhupada, carried Chaitanya’s devotion around the world.</p><p>Modern reformers drew on the saints. Mohandas <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a>’s favourite hymn, “Vaishnava Jana To”, was written by the 15th-century Gujarati poet Narsinh Mehta, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/b-r-ambedkar">B. R. Ambedkar</a> dedicated his book The Untouchables (1948) to three saints born among the untouchables, Nandanar, Ravidas and Chokhamela. Kabir’s poems, in English versions by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabindranath-tagore">Rabindranath Tagore</a> and later poets, found readers far beyond India.</p><p>Historians now question whether there was a single bhakti movement at all. The image of one wave of devotion born in the south and carried north is old: the Bhagavata Mahatmya, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> text in praise of the Bhagavata Purana, personifies Bhakti as a woman who was born in the Dravida country, grew up in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> and grew old before being made young again at Vrindavan. Scholars such as John Stratton Hawley argue that the modern idea of a pan-Indian movement took shape in the 20th century, partly to serve a national history, and stress the variety of regional traditions, some of which arose in the north on their own. Others, such as Karen Pechilis Prentiss, criticise the picture of bhakti, formed in colonial times, as a monotheistic reform against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> orthodoxy, and many now see it as a reworking of older <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> traditions rather than a revolt against them. Historians still debate how far the saints challenged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a>, and how the voices of the women saints were remembered and reshaped.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The bhakti movement, which began among Tamil poet-saints around the 6th century and spread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> over the next thousand years, taught that loving devotion to a personal god leads to liberation and is open to all. Its saints, women and men of every <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a>, sang in India’s spoken languages, founding literatures and religious communities that still shape <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> devotion today.</em></p>

